Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The best prior strategy got strong membership accuracy but poor syntax, and
// the diagnostics show why: every example had constrained activity, yet no
// example ended with a final visible answer span; instead, all answers came
// from nonfinal valid chunks and every visible close was unmatched. That points
// to a structural issue in span handling rather than just token preference.
// 
// This revision keeps the successful context-triggered opening and token-by-
// token outside generation, but makes a real structural change inside spans:
// it avoids immediately closing as soon as the parser first reports completeness.
// Instead, once the constrained prefix is complete, the strategy asks for one
// more constrained token. If EOS is chosen, it then closes the span visibly;
// if a non-EOS parser-valid token is chosen, it appends that token and keeps
// going. This should reduce premature tiny complete chunks and increase the
// chance that the final visible answer span is the actual chemistry answer.
// 
// To further improve chemical syntax, early constrained decoding uses group-
// boosted hard masking, then switches to repetition-penalized hard masking once
// the span has some content. This preserves parser validity while discouraging
// short repetitive malformed fragments that were common in the invalid outputs.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the outside-span free-token branch, either we remain
//   outside, or we observe "<<" and call EnterObservedConstrainedSpan, which
//   sets currentConstrainedOut := [], valid by parser.IsValidPrefix([]). In the
//   context-triggered open branch, OpenConstrainedSpan likewise enters with an
//   empty valid constrained prefix. In the active constrained branches,
//   GroupBoostedConstrainedStep and SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep return EOS or a
